{title: When I Survey the Wondrous Cross}
{lyricist: Isaac Watts}
{composer: Lowell Mason}
{year: 1707}
{meta: tune-year 1824}
{key: G}
{meta: tune HAMBURG}
{meta: meter LM (8.8.8.8)}
{meta: variation The hymn, sung to HAMBURG}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: tags cross, communion, Lent}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
When [G]I sur[C]vey the [G]wondrous [D]cross
On [G]which the [C]Prince of [G]glory [D]died,
My [G]richest [Em]gain I [C]count but [D]loss,
And [G]pour con[C]tempt [D]on all my [G]pride.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
For[G]bid it, [C]Lord, that [G]I should [D]boast,
Save [G]in the [C]death of [G]Christ my [D]God!
All [G]the vain [Em]things that [C]charm me [D]most,
I [G]sacri[C]fice [D]them to His [G]blood.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
See, [G]from His [C]head, His [G]hands, His [D]feet,
Sor[G]row and [C]love flow [G]mingled [D]down!
Did [G]e'er such [Em]love and [C]sorrow [D]meet,
Or [G]thorns com[C]pose [D]so rich a [G]crown?
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Were [G]the whole [C]realm of [G]nature [D]mine,
That [G]were a [C]present [G]far too [D]small;
Love [G]so a[Em]mazing, [C]so di[D]vine,
De[G]mands my [C]soul, [D]my life, my [G]all.
{end_of_verse}
